## 6. Source Strategy

### Secondary sources

Cross-national indexes and databases, including the [Tortoise's Global AI Index](https://www.tortoisemedia.com/data/global-ai#rankings), the[ Stanford Global AI Vibrancy Tool](Stanford Global AI Vibrancy Tool), the [Government AI Readiness Index](https://oxfordinsights.com/ai-readiness/government-ai-readiness-index-2025/), and comparable datasets recommended by the [OECD.AI Policy Observatory](https://oecd.ai/en/) and the [OECD.AI Index](https://www.oecd.org/en/publications/oecd-ai-observatory-index_32c01014-en.html), may be used to establish comparative evidence concerning national capabilities and structural conditions. These sources provide comparative evidence and may also support source identification and triangulation. However, index scores and rankings should not automatically be treated as evidence of specific capability mobilisation, coordination, institutional effects, or governance leverage.e.

### Primary sources

Primary sources should be prioritised for national policy positions, international positions, coordination processes, joint initiatives, declarations, agreements, standards, regulations, evaluation frameworks, monitoring or reporting arrangements, institutional changes, and observable governance outcomes.

National AI strategies and official institutional documents may be used both to establish the national capability profile and to identify how governments seek to organise or mobilise those capabilities. [The OECD.AI Policy Navigator](https://oecd.ai/en/dashboards/overview), [Digital Policy Alert](https://digitalpolicyalert.org/), and [Regulations.ai](https://regulations.ai/) be used primarily as a source-identification and discovery tool, with underlying primary documents retrieved and examined where available.

